#?(:kotoba nil :default (defn desugar-map
  "Lower a bounded literal into an owned map KIR operation.

  KEYWORD keys keep the legacy untagged pair-map (`map-new`) they have always
  had, byte for byte -- that is the representation `map-get` / `map-assoc` and
  every `match` map pattern are written against.

  `:i64` and `:string` keys lower to the canonical typed map instead. Until
  2026-09-02 they were refused here (`map keys must be bounded keywords`) and
  `kotoba-lang`'s stdlib recorded `frequencies` and `get-in` as unwritable for
  exactly that reason, even though `typed-map-new` had been generic in its key
  type the whole time -- the refusal was in the LITERAL, not in the map.

  The VALUE half was the same defect one layer over. It was fixed at `:i64`
  here, so `{1 \"a\"}` was refused with `expression type mismatch: expected
  i64, got string` -- a generic type error on a program that reads correct --
  while `(typed-map-new [:map :i64 :string] 1 \"a\")` had worked the whole time.
  Measured 2026-09-03 on sema df383ba0: the canonical surface already carried
  `:string`, `:bool`, `:keyword`, record, nested-map, heterogeneous-vector and
  typed-set values through `typed-map-new`, `typed-map-get`, `assoc`,
  `dissoc`, `contains?` and `typed-map-entry-at`, and
  `kotoba.kir.value/bounded-typed-value!` validated every one of them. Only
  the literal was pinned. `map-literal-item-type` now reads the value type off
  the literal's own value expressions.

  KEYWORD keys with a value that SPELLS a non-i64 type lower to a closed
  record instead (`keyword-map-literal-record-fields`): `{:runtime :nbb
  :compiler \"amu\" :ok? true}` is `(record-new [:record
  :kotoba.map-literal/compiler+ok?+runtime [[:compiler :string] [:ok? :bool]
  [:runtime :keyword]]] \"amu\" true :nbb)`, exactly what a `defrecord`
  constructor lowers to, so `(:compiler m)`, `get` and destructuring already
  read it with the field's static type. Until 2026-09-11 that literal was
  refused by name and pointed at `typed-map-new` -- which is homogeneous in V
  and could not have held it either. The refusal described the pair map, not
  a property of the language: `lang/surface-status.edn` carried it as
  `:implemented-partial` / `:keyword-keys :not-inferred`, and the record layer
  underneath already validated every one of these field types (measured
  2026-09-11 on the KIR interpreter: `:string`, `:bool`, `:keyword`, `:f64`,
  `:map`, `:document` fields all construct and project). A keyword-keyed
  literal whose spelled values are ALL `:i64` is untouched, byte for byte.

  Entries are emitted in the key type's total order (`compare-typed-values` in
  `kotoba.kir.value` is the same order the runtime keeps the entry chain in),
  so the KIR is reproducible without hashing identity. A key kind with no
  portable identity is refused BY NAME rather than falling through a generic
  arm, because `1.5` and `:a` fail here for entirely different reasons.

  A keyword-keyed literal in a position whose type is already a record (a
  declared record result, a record field) lowers against THAT record; see
  `desugar-map-against-record`."
  ([form] (desugar-map form nil))
  ([form contextual-type]
  (let [_ #?(:kotoba (require-k (not (> (count form) max-list-items)) "map entry count exceeds admission limit" "kotoba.error/subset-reject" form) :default (when (> (count form) max-list-items)
    (reject! "map entry count exceeds admission limit" form)))]
  (let [kinds (into #{} (map map-literal-key-kind) (keys form))
        context-record (let [t (canonical-closure-result-type contextual-type)]
                         (when (record-type? t) t))]
    (cond
      (and context-record (contains? #{#{:keyword} #{}} kinds))
      (desugar-map-against-record form context-record)

      ;; ADR 0353 floor :empty-map-literal: `{}` where its context declares
      ;; a typed map is that map, empty.
      (and (empty? form) (canonical-typed-map-type? contextual-type))
      (list 'typed-map-new contextual-type)

      (contains? kinds nil)
      (let [offender (first (remove map-literal-key-kind (keys form)))]
        (if (f64-literal-key? offender)
          #?(:kotoba (throw (fe-error (str "map literal keys must be keywords, integers or strings; "
                        "this literal has a floating point key, which has no "
                        "portable key identity -- NaN compares unequal to "
                        "itself, and +0.0 and -0.0 are equal while differing "
                        "in bits, so neither the entry order nor duplicate-key "
                        "detection is decidable") "kotoba.error/map-literal-key" form)) :default (reject! (str "map literal keys must be keywords, integers or strings; "
